@page
@model AboutModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "About";
}

<div class="gh-pagehead">
    <div>
        <h1>About this app</h1>
        <div class="sub">What GitHub Copilot Credit Visibility does, and how to read the numbers in GitHub's own terms.</div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- What is this -->
<div class="gh-card">
    <div class="ch"><h2>What is this?</h2></div>
    <div class="gh-cardbody">
        <p>
            <strong>GitHub Copilot Credit Visibility</strong> reports your organization's spend on
            <strong>GitHub Copilot</strong> — the AI pair-programmer in the IDE, on github.com and in the CLI.
            It shows <strong>AI-credit usage</strong> per user, per model and per cost center — across
            <strong>one or more GitHub enterprises</strong> — with month-over-month trends and budget alerts. With several
            enterprises registered, cost centers are always enterprise-qualified (two enterprises can both have an
            "Engineering"), an enterprise filter appears in the header, and the same person licensed in two enterprises
            is shown once per enterprise, matching how GitHub bills.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-0" style="padding:10px 14px;background:#eef4ff;border:1px solid #dbe6ff;border-radius:10px;">
            ⚠️ <strong>This is GitHub Copilot — not Microsoft 365 Copilot.</strong> The two are separate products with separate billing.
            All figures here come from GitHub's billing platform (the 🐙 octocat mark in the header denotes GitHub), and reflect
            Copilot <em>AI-credit</em> usage — not Microsoft 365 Copilot seats or Azure spend.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- What each page shows -->
<div class="gh-grid">
    <div>
        <div class="gh-card">
            <div class="ch"><h2>Usage</h2></div>
            <div class="gh-cardbody">
                <p class="mb-0">
                    The <a asp-page="/Index">Usage</a> page shows a chosen month: headline KPIs (total spend, active users,
                    cost centers, average per user), per-user net spend with month-over-month change, the split by cost center
                    and by model, and <strong>budgets needing attention</strong> (at scale only exceptions are shown — the
                    complete list lives on the <a asp-page="/Budgets">Budgets</a> page, filterable by enterprise and status).
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="gh-card">
            <div class="ch"><h2>Reports</h2></div>
            <div class="gh-cardbody">
                <p class="mb-0">
                    The <a asp-page="/Reports">Reports</a> page is the trend workbench. Group spend by <strong>total, user, model,
                    cost center or enterprise</strong>, bucket it by <strong>day, week or month</strong>, then pin optional filters
                    that <em>compose</em> — e.g. one user broken down by model over the last 90 days, or one enterprise's cost
                    centers over a year — as a chart or a table.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div>
        <div class="gh-card">
            <div class="ch"><h2>Cost centers</h2></div>
            <div class="gh-cardbody">
                <p class="mb-0">
                    Cost centers group spend by team, department or budget line. On the <a asp-page="/Reports">Reports</a> page choose
                    <strong>Group by → Cost center</strong> to see spend and trends per cost center; the Usage page also shows the
                    current-month cost-center split.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="gh-card">
            <div class="ch"><h2>Admin</h2></div>
            <div class="gh-cardbody">
                <p class="mb-0">
                    The <strong>Admin</strong> console (admins only) manages the <strong>GitHub enterprise registry</strong> (add,
                    enable/disable or remove enterprises — a runtime operation, no redeploy), sets the organization name, maps Entra
                    groups/users to enterprise-qualified GitHub cost centers (who sees what), designates administrators, and manages
                    <strong>budgets &amp; alert thresholds</strong>.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- Understanding the data (GitHub terms + verified links) -->
<div class="gh-card">
    <div class="ch"><h2>Understanding the data — in GitHub's terms</h2><span class="hint">links open official GitHub Docs</span></div>
    <div class="gh-cardbody">
        <dl style="margin:0;display:grid;grid-template-columns:auto 1fr;gap:10px 18px;">
            <dt><strong>AI credits</strong></dt>
            <dd style="margin:0">
                Copilot usage is measured in <em>AI credits</em> — <strong>1 credit = $0.01 USD</strong>, consumed at each
                model's token rate (input, output and cached tokens). Every plan includes a monthly credit allowance;
                only consumption beyond it is billable. Code completions and next-edit suggestions aren't billed.
                <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/billing/concepts/product-billing/github-copilot-billing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Copilot billing ↗</a>
            </dd>

            <dt><strong>Gross vs. net</strong></dt>
            <dd style="margin:0">
                <em>Gross</em> is what was consumed; <em>net</em> is what's billable once the included allowance is applied.
                A team still inside its allowance shows a net of <strong>$0 while genuinely using Copilot</strong> — which is why
                this app shows consumption alongside billable spend. A $0 total labelled
                <em>"fully covered by allowance"</em> means healthy usage that happens to cost nothing; it does
                <strong>not</strong> mean nobody used Copilot.
            </dd>

            <dt><strong>Premium requests <span class="hint">(legacy)</span></strong></dt>
            <dd style="margin:0">
                The <em>previous</em> billing model, replaced by AI credits on <strong>1 June 2026</strong>. It now applies only to
                individual Copilot Pro / Pro+ subscribers who remained on an existing annual plan — not to enterprises.
                If you're comparing against figures from before that date, they're in a different unit and aren't
                directly comparable.
                <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/copilot-billing/request-based-billing-legacy/copilot-requests" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Premium requests (legacy) ↗</a>
            </dd>

            <dt><strong>Per-user usage</strong></dt>
            <dd style="margin:0">
                GitHub's detailed usage report breaks spend down by user, product, SKU, model and unit type, including a cost-center field.
                <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/billing/reference/billing-reports" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Billing reports reference ↗</a>
            </dd>

            <dt><strong>Cost centers</strong></dt>
            <dd style="margin:0">
                Cost centers group organizations/users so spend can be attributed to a team, department or budget line.
                <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@@latest/billing/concepts/cost-centers" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cost centers ↗</a>
            </dd>

            <dt><strong>Budgets &amp; alerts</strong></dt>
            <dd style="margin:0">
                GitHub supports budgets with alert thresholds at the enterprise, organization, cost-center and user levels. The budgets on
                the Usage dashboard mirror this model.
                <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@@latest/billing/concepts/budgets-and-alerts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Budgets &amp; alerts ↗</a> ·
                <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/billing/budgets-for-usage-based-billing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copilot usage-based budgets ↗</a>
            </dd>

            <dt><strong>Plans &amp; product billing</strong></dt>
            <dd style="margin:0">
                <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/get-started/plans" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copilot plans ↗</a> ·
                <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/billing/concepts/product-billing/github-copilot" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copilot product billing ↗</a>
            </dd>

            <dt><strong>Data source (API)</strong></dt>
            <dd style="margin:0">
                Usage and budgets are read from the GitHub billing REST API, persisted to a database, and served here (the UI never calls
                GitHub live per page-load).
                <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/rest/billing?apiVersion=2022-11-28" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Billing REST API ↗</a> ·
                <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/rest/billing/budgets" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Budgets REST API ↗</a>
            </dd>
        </dl>
    </div>
</div>
